{
  "name": "regex-invalid-groups-property-names",
  "description": "A pattern Dart's RegExp refuses makes the `regex` transform resolve to null (the client catches the error), so these rows resolve to null. With `g`, the `(?` forms that are not a well-formed modifier group: `(?i)a` (a modifier with no colon), `(?-:a)` (nothing added or removed), `(?x:a)` (x is not a modifier) and `(?ii:a)` (the same modifier twice). With `gu`, property names outside ECMAScript's tables: `\\p{IsLatin}` (a Java prefix), `\\p{Han}` (a script value without `Script=`), `\\p{script=latin}` (names and values match exactly, case included) and `\\pL` (no braces). With `gu` the long General_Category names, the `gc=` form and the binary properties are accepted; on \"aB1\u00e9\u4e2d\ud83d\ude00\" `\\p{Lowercase_Letter}` gives \"#B1#\u4e2d\ud83d\ude00\", `\\p{gc=Cased_Letter}` gives \"##1#\u4e2d\ud83d\ude00\", `\\p{Alphabetic}` gives \"##1##\ud83d\ude00\" (the ideograph U+4E2D is alphabetic but not a cased letter), `\\p{Any}` gives \"######\" (the emoji is one code point) and `[^\\p{Lowercase_Letter}]` gives \"a##\u00e9##\". Every replace-mode regex puts \"#\" for each match. Every input reads page state, so the engine keeps the Values unresolved with `watches` and the client resolves them.",
  "input": {
    "type": "Column",
    "key": "col",
    "children": [
      {
        "type": "Text",
        "key": "inline_i",
        "props": {
